      <!-- 30-90 DAYS -->
      <div class="forecast-bucket-label">30–90 Days</div>

      <!-- F5: iaea-physical-access-iran-by-sep-15 (52d) -->
      <div class="forecast-card cat-diplomacy status-ACTIVE">
        <div class="fc-head">
          <span class="fc-id">F5 · 2026-07-25-iaea-physical-access-iran-by-sep-15</span>
          <span class="fc-category">diplomacy</span>
        </div>
        <div class="fc-question">Will the IAEA publicly confirm that its inspection team has physically entered Iran and begun verification activities at at least one declared Iranian nuclear facility before 2026-09-15?</div>
        <div class="fc-probrow">
          <span class="fc-p">35%</span>
          <span class="fc-delta no-move">Δ 0pp · new forecast (created 2026-07-25)</span>
          <span class="fc-horizon">→ resolves by 2026-09-15</span>
        </div>
        <div class="fc-delta-reason">IAEA Director General Rafael Grossi stated July 25, 2026 that Iran has invited an inspection team to visit 'within weeks' — the first such invitation since IAEA withdrew from Iran in the conflict's early phase. Iran has previously extended IAEA invitations and then cancelled or restricted delegations; the active US bombing campaign and July 9 MOU collapse create a volatile environment in which Iran may use the invitation as diplomatic leverage without following through. The 0.35 probability credits the real development while accounting for Iran's track record of inspection access management.</div>
        <dl class="fc-meta-row">
          <dt>Resolution</dt><dd>YES if IAEA Director General or official spokesperson states in a public communication that IAEA inspectors physically entered Iran and conducted or commenced at least one monitoring or verification visit at a facility covered under Iran's Comprehensive Safeguards Agreement before 2026-09-15. YES applies even if inspectors are subsequently expelled, provided at least one site visit commenced.</dd>
          <dt>Sources OK</dt><dd>IAEA official statement at iaea.org (primary), OR Reuters/AP citing IAEA Director General Rafael Grossi directly in on-record quoted speech confirming physical entry and site access.</dd>
          <dt>Ambiguity</dt><dd>An IAEA DG visit to Tehran for diplomatic talks, without physical access to declared facilities, = AMBIGUOUS-NO. IAEA satellite imagery analysis is NOT physical access. A 'goodwill visit' with camera access at a facility gate without entry to monitoring instruments = AMBIGUOUS-NO.</dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="fc-indicators">
          <span class="fc-indicator state-NOT_OBSERVED">Grossi confirms travel date to named Iranian facility</span>
          <span class="fc-indicator state-NOT_OBSERVED">Iranian AEOI confirms IAEA delegation arrival</span>
          <span class="fc-indicator state-NOT_OBSERVED">IAEA Board of Governors interim Iran report citing inspection visits</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- F6: us-iran-formal-ceasefire-by-oct-1 (68d) -->
      <div class="forecast-card cat-diplomacy status-ACTIVE">
        <div class="fc-head">
          <span class="fc-id">F6 · 2026-07-25-us-iran-formal-ceasefire-by-oct-1</span>
          <span class="fc-category">diplomacy</span>
        </div>
        <div class="fc-question">Will the United States and Iran sign a formally documented ceasefire agreement (or mutually confirmed interim deal) before EOD 2026-10-01?</div>
        <div class="fc-probrow">
          <span class="fc-p">35%</span>
          <span class="fc-delta no-move">Δ 0pp · new forecast (created 2026-07-25)</span>
          <span class="fc-horizon">→ resolves by 2026-10-01</span>
        </div>
        <div class="fc-delta-reason">The conflict has cycled through two ceasefire-then-collapse sequences (April 8 ceasefire, June 17 MOU). As of July 25: 13+ consecutive nights of US strikes; Iran rejected the Iraqi PM proposal; neither side accepted the Qatar-Pakistan 10-day halt. The 68-day window through Oct 1 allows time for accumulated economic and military pressure to drive a third ceasefire attempt. The June 17 MOU cycle (from equivalent impasse to signed deal in 16 days) is the structural precedent. Active mediation channels (Qatar, Pakistan, potentially Oman) remain open. SecDef Hegseth confirmed $37.5B spent through July 22 at $2-3B/week, providing domestic fiscal incentive to close a deal before the Oct 27 Israeli election.</div>
        <dl class="fc-meta-row">
          <dt>Resolution</dt><dd>YES if a formal written ceasefire agreement or interim deal is signed by named US and Iranian representatives (or publicly acknowledged in joint statement by named mediator hosting both sides), with both sides confirming cessation of offensive strikes. The June 17 MOU format qualifies. A unilateral pause does not qualify.</dd>
          <dt>Sources OK</dt><dd>TIER1 outlet publishing the text or terms of the agreement with attribution to named US and Iranian officials. Qatar FM, Pakistan PM, or Oman FM confirming the signing as named mediator = sufficient.</dd>
          <dt>Ambiguity</dt><dd>If fighting stops for ≥5 days but no document is signed, resolve AMBIGUOUS (count as NO — the question requires formal documentation). If one side claims an agreement and the other denies it, resolve NO.</dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="fc-indicators">
          <span class="fc-indicator state-PARTIAL">Qatar mediation active — 10-day proposal (July 20) not yet accepted</span>
          <span class="fc-indicator state-OBSERVED">US $37.5B war spend through July 22 (fiscal pressure building)</span>
          <span class="fc-indicator state-OBSERVED">Hormuz MCM progress — southern corridor widening</span>
        </div>
