  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On July 23, 2026, Houthi forces declared a formal naval blockade against Saudi Arabia, Israeli-linked, and US-associated commercial shipping in the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden. The announcement was accompanied by two confirmed strikes on Saudi oil tankers — one set ablaze in the Gulf of Aden — the first time the group has named Saudi Arabia as an explicit blockade target. Brent crude closed at $101.10/barrel on July 23, the first $100+ close since late May, before retreating to $97–98 on July 24 following US Strategic Petroleum Reserve release confirmations. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>The July 9 collapse of the June 17 MOU ceasefire entered its third week without resolution. US air and naval strikes continued without interruption: CENTCOM counted 13 or more consecutive nights of precision strikes on IRGC, NIOC, and infrastructure targets as of July 25. The Trump administration confirmed on July 24 that strikes will continue; Iran formally rejected an Iraqi PM-brokered ceasefire proposal; and neither side has accepted the Qatar and Pakistan 10-day halt-in-place proposal circulated July 20. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>IAEA Director General Rafael Grossi stated on July 25 that Iran has extended an invitation for an inspection team to visit 'within weeks' — the first such invitation since IAEA withdrew from Iran in the conflict's early phase. Grossi did not specify a facility name, a confirmed travel date, or a written commitment from Iran's Atomic Energy Organization. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>A Chatham House analysis published July 2026 assessed the Iran war as having "failed to deliver the decisive victory many Israelis expected." The Knesset dissolved July 17 following a 62-0 vote on the self-dissolution bill, after UTJ's departure reduced the coalition to 48 seats — below the 61-seat majority threshold. Channel 13 polling (July 2026) projects 51 seats for the Netanyahu bloc and 58 for the center-left opposition ahead of the October 27 election. → supports <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

